# The engineering loop — one iteration, continuously

*Rendered by `uel init` from the UEL loop template. This is the standing
protocol for autonomous sessions working `{{PROJECT_REL}}`: wake, run one
iteration against the model, record what it cost, go away. Knowledge survives
in the graph, the lock, the overlays, and the issue tracker — never in the
session. Edit it to fit your program; keep the shape.*

| parameter | this instance |
|---|---|
| model | `{{PROJECT_REL}}` |
| work branches | `{{BRANCH_PREFIX}}<UTC date>` |
| cost log | `docs/org/loop-log.md` |
| skill | `uel-loop` (`.claude/skills/uel-loop/`) |

## Who runs this

An autonomous agent session, woken by a schedule (daily is a reasonable start)
or by an event: a push, a landed measurement campaign, a filed issue. Sessions
share no context with each other — that is the point. The graph is the memory;
this runbook is the procedure; both outlive every context window that touches
them.

## The iteration

1. **Sync.** Fetch and check out the default branch.
2. **Read the agenda.** `uel agenda {{PROJECT_REL}} --json`. Its section order
   is the work policy:
   1. compile errors — nothing outranks a red check;
   2. stale work in attention order (`»` = frontier, buildable now);
   3. pending judgments — computed but unjudged results are epistemic debt;
   4. discrepancies, investigation stubs, entailment candidates — reality
      disagreed with a model; resolving that is itself an analysis;
   5. the top `info-value` measurement — draft the campaign that captures it;
   6. tightest budget — find what moved before margin becomes a crisis.
3. **Do the top item, completely.** One item done beats five touched.
4. **Gate.** `uel check` clean, `uel build` to freshness, `uel fmt`. Never
   commit a red graph.
5. **Record.** Commit to `{{BRANCH_PREFIX}}<UTC date>`, message naming the item
   taken and the judgment reached. Append one line to `docs/org/loop-log.md`:
   date, item, outcome, minutes.
6. **File.** Anything that blocked, surprised, or cost the session becomes a
   typed issue with provenance — what happened, which command, repro, cost.
   Kernel defects go upstream as issues, never local patches.

## Why the log matters

The claim under all of this is that each design-build-test cycle makes the next
one cheaper. That claim is empirical, and it is only measurable if iterations
happen *and* are counted. The loop-log is the denominator: cost per merged
claim. Two consecutive iterations with no improvement is not a disappointment —
it is data, and it should change what the program does next.

## Kill switch

The schedule is the only moving part; disable or delete it and the loop stops
while the protocol stays. Whoever funds the compute holds that switch.
